Landscape 0.26.3 + e9f019b5: keep IPv6 working on a WAN link with no bound
IPv6 address (the E5's modem uplink).

The E5's uplink is sipa_eth0, the modem's SIP netdev. netifd configures it
from a bearer whose IPv6 method is dhcp and which yields only a link-local
address, so nothing ever inserts an IPv6 row into Landscape's wan_ip_binding
map for ifindex 3. Every IPv6 packet that reaches the TC data path then hits
`wan_ip_info == NULL` in landscape-ebpf/src/bpf/nat/tc_nat6.h and is dropped
with TC_ACT_SHOT -- the router's own traffic (ping6, DNS, DHCPv6) as well as
everything it forwards. Measured on the device: 200 forwarded datagrams left
Ip6OutForwDatagrams +200 while sipa_eth0 tx_packets did not move, and the
router itself could not reach any IPv6 address at all.

There is no WAN prefix to translate to in that case, so the NAT stage has
nothing to do and must let the packet continue to the kernel route. The two
route stages get the same treatment for the default flow only: a default flow
with no Landscape target falls through to the kernel route (a carrier-assigned
/64 with no PD pool is addressed by the LAN RA server), while an explicit
flow whose target is missing still fails closed.

The eBPF tests change with it: the no-binding NAT cases are new
(landscape-ebpf/src/tests/nat/v6/no_wan_binding.rs) and the two route suites
now expect the default flow to reach the kernel route. `cargo ttb` runs them.

Apply with: patch -p1 (or git apply) in a checkout of the pinned commit.

